{
 "preset": "flat-space",
 "topic": "What if Earth had rings?",
 "lines": [
  {
   "text": "Imagine looking up at night and seeing a glowing bridge of ice arching across the sky.",
   "start": 0.4,
   "end": 6.4
  },
  {
   "text": "Earth almost had rings like this, and it still might one day.",
   "start": 6.75,
   "end": 11.25
  },
  {
   "text": "A ring is born when a moon wanders too close and gravity tears it into billions of pieces.",
   "start": 11.6,
   "end": 18.35
  },
  {
   "text": "Those pieces spread into a disk that is wide but astonishingly thin.",
   "start": 18.7,
   "end": 23.2
  },
  {
   "text": "From the equator, the ring would be a razor-thin line slicing the sky in two.",
   "start": 23.549999999999997,
   "end": 29.174999999999997
  },
  {
   "text": "Further north, it would cast a shadow belt across entire countries.",
   "start": 29.525,
   "end": 33.65
  },
  {
   "text": "Seasons would shift, and so would the light that plants and animals depend on.",
   "start": 34,
   "end": 39.25
  },
  {
   "text": "One collision could rewrite the sky over our heads.",
   "start": 39.6,
   "end": 42.975
  }
 ],
